00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:12.690
You probably all agree that
security-- good security--

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:14.380
comes from the layers.

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:17.250
There is no, again,
defense in depth.

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:20.290
It's very common things
that everybody understood.

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:23.010
And that's really
why we actually

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:27.630
started to harden our systems
starting from the bottom,

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:31.860
from the lowest level we can go.

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:36.360
And you probably heard
enough today about Titan.

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:41.130
But we will repeat again why and
what Titan is providing for us.

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:45.750
So Titan is a security
chip that Google designed

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:47.790
and Google developed.

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:52.350
And these Titan
security chips help

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:55.830
us to provide very
important security controls

00:03:55.830 --> 00:03:58.180
for our infrastructure.

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:01.530
So the first one that will
be applicable to this talk

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:03.660
is machine identity.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:07.080
And machine identity
is done by the virtue

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:10.740
of how we manufacture Titan.

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:14.180
When we manufacture Titan,
the first things that we do,

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:18.660
we have set of keys that
we infuse in the Titan.

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:23.640
They burn it into the fuses
so they are not extractable.

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:28.440
We harvest those keys
in our registry and also

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:29.670
its Titan registry.

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:34.890
And we issue certificates, 509
certificates, to those Titans.

00:04:34.890 --> 00:04:37.740
And now what
happened, those Titans

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:40.560
can be challenged or asserted.

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:43.440
And they can explain or
cryptographically prove

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:46.020
that they have
possession of these keys,

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:48.000
those Titan identity keys.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:51.720
So now imagine that you put
this key on the motherboard

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:54.240
or network card,
and this motherboard

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:56.790
you put in the server.

00:04:56.790 --> 00:05:00.940
Now this server can actually
cryptographically prove

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:03.900
that this server is belong
to Google in standing

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:08.420
in the right data center
and provide any capabilities

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:09.720
that we expect from servers.

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:13.330
So we called it
machine identity.

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:16.570
This second very
important property

00:05:16.570 --> 00:05:20.740
and control that Titan's
offered to us is secure boot.

00:05:20.740 --> 00:05:22.900
So secure boot,
again, probably it's

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:24.730
well known, standard concept.

00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:26.500
You probably know about that.

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:29.620
Otherwise you will not
come to our presentation.

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:35.170
But secure boot is the idea
that every single binary

00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:37.150
in your boot sequence
needs to be signed

00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:38.890
by appropriate authority.

00:05:38.890 --> 00:05:41.420
And if it will figure out
that something is not signed,

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:47.090
we will stop your boot and
boot of our system as well.

00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:51.710
Also, again, the interesting
thing with secure boot,

00:05:51.710 --> 00:05:57.190
we are trying to
provide the highest

00:05:57.190 --> 00:06:00.160
security with our secure boot.

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:02.800
And what we call
this capability,

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:05.920
it's called first
instruction integrity.

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:08.980
And despite of long name,
it means very simple thing.

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:11.370
It means before you
execute anything,

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:15.400
let's ensure that it was not
tampered with or modified.

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:18.780
And that's what we
do with our firmware.

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:22.660
So Titan, when you
power up your server,

00:06:22.660 --> 00:06:24.900
is actually validating
their own firmware.

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:28.210
It's not trusting
itself blindly.

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:30.370
And after that,
it's trying to help

00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:33.950
to validate the firmware
of the machine itself.

00:06:33.950 --> 00:06:36.640
It does it because it has
very important two properties.

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:40.100
First of all, strategic
location-- location, location,

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:40.900
location.

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:45.130
And what Titan is, it's sitting
in between the flash that

00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:48.730
has this firmware and
platform controller hub that's

00:06:48.730 --> 00:06:50.950
actually reading this firmware.

00:06:50.950 --> 00:06:54.340
And the second interesting
property that Titan has,

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:57.610
it can keep your CPU at reset.

00:06:57.610 --> 00:07:00.310
And it means every
single beat that PCH

00:07:00.310 --> 00:07:03.580
is reading from the flash
is going through the Titan.

00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:08.680
Titan validated, and only
after validation is complete,

00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:11.320
it removes CPU from
reset, releases it.

00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:15.190
And now CPU can execute
these instructions.

00:07:15.190 --> 00:07:19.300
So this capability we called
first instruction integrity.

00:07:19.300 --> 00:07:22.000
And the last one, measure boot.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:25.510
And measure boot is idea
that compared to secure boot,

00:07:25.510 --> 00:07:28.780
it will provide much
more detailed information

00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:31.570
about what is happening
during boot sequence.

00:07:31.570 --> 00:07:35.350
And measure boot in
our server machines

00:07:35.350 --> 00:07:37.830
ensures that kernel
binaries that we're

00:07:37.830 --> 00:07:41.680
putting on top of our firmware
and our boot loader is not

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:44.890
tampered with or not modified.

00:07:44.890 --> 00:07:48.370
Why I'm talking
about that today?

00:07:48.370 --> 00:07:52.480
Because as you can imagine,
after we laid down our kernel

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:59.500
and validated our hypervisor
KVM, now we start it again.

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:03.660
What I mean by that, now you
bring your compute resources.

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:07.990
And now when we started
your VM, the whole cycle

00:08:07.990 --> 00:08:09.940
started from beginning.

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:12.520
We need to have your
firmware running.

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:15.310
We need to ensure that
boot loader is here.

00:08:15.310 --> 00:08:20.200
We need to run the OS, the
boot sequence, and kernel.

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:25.210
And all of that has to
be somehow validated.

00:08:25.210 --> 00:08:30.750
And today, until shielded
VM showed up, it was not.

00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:33.120
So customers ask me--

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:34.440
I'm a product manager--

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:39.240
very many times if in my VM,
somebody compromised my VM

00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:43.080
and installed some
backdoor, how do I know.

00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:49.110
And being very frank with you,
you have no idea, literally.

00:08:49.110 --> 00:08:54.840
So we want to help you to
find this very advance threat.

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:56.910
And from attackers'
perspective, they

00:08:56.910 --> 00:08:59.170
want to compromise your VM.

00:08:59.170 --> 00:09:03.660
They want to install malware
and all other boot level

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:07.824
and rootkit, the malware that's
actually incredibly dangerous.

00:09:07.824 --> 00:09:09.240
And the reason why
it's dangerous,

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:12.990
because it presides
below, beneath your OS.

00:09:12.990 --> 00:09:14.820
And when you're
starting your OS,

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:18.150
you actually have no idea
what is happening below you.

00:09:18.150 --> 00:09:20.010
And also these
threats are usually

00:09:20.010 --> 00:09:22.290
persistent between the boots.

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:26.430
It means it's has
very long lived cycle

00:09:26.430 --> 00:09:29.130
and also they are undetectable.

00:09:29.130 --> 00:09:31.590
So from our customers'
perspective,

00:09:31.590 --> 00:09:35.160
they want to starve those VMs,
those tampered VM from running,

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:39.010
figure out what is happening,
update them, page them,

00:09:39.010 --> 00:09:41.160
help them, and after
that, reestablish

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:43.290
trust with those VMs.

00:09:43.290 --> 00:09:47.400
And also, very importantly,
all secrets on those VMs

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:51.940
need to be locked to those
VMs or to the environment.

00:09:51.940 --> 00:09:57.200
And it's very important reason,
because the passwords, also

00:09:57.200 --> 00:10:00.660
anything of value, is
usually global secret.

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:02.580
And it's software
barrier secret.

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:07.350
And it means when attacker will
exfiltrate it from single VM,

00:10:07.350 --> 00:10:12.060
it can apply it through lateral
move to every VM that you have.

00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:14.460
And it's a really,
really bad idea.

00:10:14.460 --> 00:10:17.310
So what and how we
can help with this--

00:10:17.310 --> 00:10:20.150
and it's [INAUDIBLE]
yesterday not today,

00:10:20.150 --> 00:10:23.700
we introduced public
beta of shielded VMs.

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:27.080
And shielded VMs
is a product that

00:10:27.080 --> 00:10:30.480
helps to protect against
those advanced threats

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:34.380
on your compute resource level.

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:38.670
We want to provide, and we're
providing, verifiable integrity

00:10:38.670 --> 00:10:40.740
and exfiltration resistance.

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:44.820
And we offer it configured by
default, enabled by default,

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:46.700
and free of charge.

00:10:46.700 --> 00:10:49.595
So the questions that I'm
receiving every single time,

00:10:49.595 --> 00:10:52.900
if it's so great thing, why not?

00:10:52.900 --> 00:10:55.260
And the answer
is, please use it.

00:10:55.260 --> 00:11:00.010
It's actually a lot of value
for you, for our customers.

00:11:00.010 --> 00:11:01.740
So what can you do with that?

00:11:01.740 --> 00:11:04.410
Let me explain to
you a little more.

00:11:04.410 --> 00:11:11.430
So we enabled on your VM, we
replace your old and rusty BIOS

00:11:11.430 --> 00:11:15.000
or your master boot record,
and replaced it with unified

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:16.830
extended firmware interfaces.

00:11:16.830 --> 00:11:18.671
Firmware, it's wonderful.

00:11:18.671 --> 00:11:19.545
It's very extensible.

00:11:19.545 --> 00:11:22.800
We're trying to not make
it super extensible.

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:25.590
But a few extensions
are very important.

00:11:25.590 --> 00:11:28.140
And this extension
is secure boot.

00:11:28.140 --> 00:11:30.540
So we're enabling
secure boot, similarly

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:32.730
to what we have in
our infrastructure

00:11:32.730 --> 00:11:36.240
for your VM for a
specific subset of Google

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:38.610
curated images.

00:11:38.610 --> 00:11:42.180
In addition to that, before
I will go any farther--

00:11:42.180 --> 00:11:46.120
in addition to that, we enabled
virtual trusted platform

00:11:46.120 --> 00:11:47.770
module.

00:11:47.770 --> 00:11:52.050
It's a very long name but
it's crypto engine that's

00:11:52.050 --> 00:11:53.940
very similar to our Titan.

00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:55.370
But it's virtual device.

00:11:55.370 --> 00:11:58.910
It's software that every
VM will have their own.

00:11:58.910 --> 00:12:02.100
And VTPMs, or Virtual
Trusted Platform Modules

00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:06.210
can do your crypto outside
of boundary of your VM.

00:12:06.210 --> 00:12:09.090
It means any vulnerability
of your kernel or user

00:12:09.090 --> 00:12:12.270
mode in your VM will
not touch, will not

00:12:12.270 --> 00:12:14.590
change your
cryptographic operation

00:12:14.590 --> 00:12:16.740
that VTPM perform for you.

00:12:16.740 --> 00:12:21.540
And VTPMs are actually
the normal crypto engine.

00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:23.820
It can generate the keys.

00:12:23.820 --> 00:12:26.020
It can protect the keys.

00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:30.390
And it can test the keys that
are coming from this VTPMs.

00:12:30.390 --> 00:12:33.090
And Andy will talk,
again, in details

00:12:33.090 --> 00:12:35.130
about what VTPMs can do.

00:12:35.130 --> 00:12:37.230
But one of the most
important features

00:12:37.230 --> 00:12:40.170
that our customers
like about VTPMs,

00:12:40.170 --> 00:12:45.210
now they don't need
to trust blindly VMs.

00:12:45.210 --> 00:12:47.610
Their VMs, they can
actually challenge, exactly

00:12:47.610 --> 00:12:49.410
as we challenge machines.

00:12:49.410 --> 00:12:53.880
And they can say, VTPM, prove
to me that you're actually

00:12:53.880 --> 00:12:55.980
part of this VM.

00:12:55.980 --> 00:12:59.700
And what's happening with
VTPM and its standard,

00:12:59.700 --> 00:13:02.280
again, TCG, Trusted
Computing Groups [INAUDIBLE]..

00:13:02.280 --> 00:13:07.320
So it's absolutely standard CPM
2.0 interface that we enabled.

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:11.290
We're becoming TPM
vendors as well.

00:13:11.290 --> 00:13:14.980
So TPM pass all compliant tests.

00:13:14.980 --> 00:13:17.810
So TPMs have very
interesting capabilities.

00:13:17.810 --> 00:13:19.710
They have this master key.

00:13:19.710 --> 00:13:21.600
It's called endorsement key.

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:27.230
And we are using our Google
CA to issue certificate

00:13:27.230 --> 00:13:29.220
to those keys.

00:13:29.220 --> 00:13:32.160
And these keys are actually
the most important thing

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:36.220
because then the VTPM can
attest if [INAUDIBLE]..

00:13:36.220 --> 00:13:40.140
So if you will challenge your
VM and say, OK, give me your--

00:13:40.140 --> 00:13:43.050
again proves that
I have this VM.

00:13:43.050 --> 00:13:46.970
In addition to the cryptographic
attestation and signature,

00:13:46.970 --> 00:13:50.070
we're giving you
triple information.

00:13:50.070 --> 00:13:52.190
And that's very
important information.

00:13:52.190 --> 00:13:57.510
It's instance ID, it's the
project ID, and the zone.

00:13:57.510 --> 00:14:02.020
And then you have cryptographic
proof that it's the VMs

00:14:02.020 --> 00:14:03.960
that you are really talking to.

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:07.620
There is no way for
anybody to change it

00:14:07.620 --> 00:14:11.910
because it's tamper evident
log that we provide to you

00:14:11.910 --> 00:14:14.100
as our attestation statement.

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:16.740
And that's what we call
strong VM identity.

00:14:16.740 --> 00:14:18.720
Based on that, you
can decide if you

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:21.600
trust this VM to
share your secrets

00:14:21.600 --> 00:14:24.030
or you are not
trusting it very much.

00:14:24.030 --> 00:14:26.160
The second thing
that we enabled,

00:14:26.160 --> 00:14:28.530
in addition to secure
boot, is measure boot,

00:14:28.530 --> 00:14:32.190
very similarly to what we have
in our infrastructure level.

00:14:32.190 --> 00:14:36.300
But in addition to what
measure boot's expected to do,

00:14:36.300 --> 00:14:40.290
utilizing this VTPM
and measuring integrity

00:14:40.290 --> 00:14:44.790
of every single binary that
we use in our boot sequence

00:14:44.790 --> 00:14:47.060
and providing much more
detailed information.

00:14:47.060 --> 00:14:51.230
For example, because how
this particular measurement

00:14:51.230 --> 00:14:54.300
is created, every single
binary, before it's

00:14:54.300 --> 00:14:58.440
executed in your pass,
is hashed and added

00:14:58.440 --> 00:15:00.870
to the previous hash of
all other binaries that

00:15:00.870 --> 00:15:02.820
have already been hashed.

00:15:02.820 --> 00:15:05.670
And when you're going
through all of these cycles,

00:15:05.670 --> 00:15:09.000
by the end of the day
you will have this hash

00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:11.940
that you can compare
with baseline.

00:15:11.940 --> 00:15:14.640
And we're creating this
baseline for your first time

00:15:14.640 --> 00:15:17.970
ever that we are running
and starting this VM.

00:15:17.970 --> 00:15:23.160
And [INAUDIBLE] runtime
calculated attestation

00:15:23.160 --> 00:15:25.970
is different from
our baseline, we're

00:15:25.970 --> 00:15:28.260
sending all of this
information to you.

00:15:28.260 --> 00:15:30.310
So you can take actions.

00:15:30.310 --> 00:15:32.190
Finally, you will not be blind.

00:15:32.190 --> 00:15:36.240
And if anything will change
during this period of time,

00:15:36.240 --> 00:15:40.380
we're sending this information
into Stackdriver notification

00:15:40.380 --> 00:15:42.780
and alarms and all
other functions.

00:15:42.780 --> 00:15:45.810
You can decide, stop
immediately this VM.

00:15:45.810 --> 00:15:49.260
Literally stop, I need to
extract it and go with forensic

00:15:49.260 --> 00:15:50.760
to figure out what is up.

00:15:50.760 --> 00:15:55.080
Because out of thousands
of those VMs, one of them,

00:15:55.080 --> 00:15:57.330
integrity failed.

00:15:57.330 --> 00:15:59.100
Not a good idea.

00:15:59.100 --> 00:16:03.870
But if you know that
this change is expected,

00:16:03.870 --> 00:16:06.120
absolutely legit, you
updated something,

00:16:06.120 --> 00:16:09.000
you added kernel driver,
you've done something

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:15.180
that you understand, you have
a way to rebuild this baseline

00:16:15.180 --> 00:16:20.820
and start measuring integrity
of your boot sequence and kernel

00:16:20.820 --> 00:16:24.300
based on this new
baseline that you trusted.

00:16:24.300 --> 00:16:26.010
It's actually very
important when

00:16:26.010 --> 00:16:29.670
you need to re-establish
your trust with this VM.

00:16:29.670 --> 00:16:32.350
If VM was compromised
and you patched it,

00:16:32.350 --> 00:16:34.410
the question is how
to re-establish trust,

00:16:34.410 --> 00:16:36.930
how to continue to
trust this machine.

00:16:36.930 --> 00:16:39.390
And we're providing
you with that.

00:16:39.390 --> 00:16:42.840
And verifiable integrity,
you can actually see that

00:16:42.840 --> 00:16:45.300
and understand and take action.

00:16:45.300 --> 00:16:49.890
And VTPMs have very good
opportunity to actually

00:16:49.890 --> 00:16:51.860
