00:24:20.846 --> 00:24:22.220
But if you're an
attacker and you

00:24:22.220 --> 00:24:24.620
want to break into something
and stay in there long term,

00:24:24.620 --> 00:24:26.120
you can lie to all these things.

00:24:26.120 --> 00:24:26.619
Right?

00:24:26.619 --> 00:24:28.880
So our threat model is your
attacker has broken in,

00:24:28.880 --> 00:24:30.860
has root privileges on your VM.

00:24:30.860 --> 00:24:32.944
So if someone has root
privileges on your VM,

00:24:32.944 --> 00:24:34.610
can you really trust
any of these things

00:24:34.610 --> 00:24:38.000
up here if you can't
trust the thing down here?

00:24:38.000 --> 00:24:40.310
So that's really what
shielded VMs is about,

00:24:40.310 --> 00:24:42.744
is making sure that once
you have this lower level

00:24:42.744 --> 00:24:44.660
and you're certain that
this isn't compromised

00:24:44.660 --> 00:24:46.520
and this is in good shape,
then you can actually

00:24:46.520 --> 00:24:48.269
have some faith in the
higher level things

00:24:48.269 --> 00:24:49.410
you have above that.

00:24:49.410 --> 00:24:52.310
So let's talk about how
that works specifically.

00:24:52.310 --> 00:24:54.860
So here's an example
of secure boot.

00:24:54.860 --> 00:24:57.500
So secure boot's been
around a long time--

00:24:57.500 --> 00:24:59.180
10 years, 20 years,
something like that.

00:24:59.180 --> 00:25:01.180
And it's been around in
the real hardware world.

00:25:01.180 --> 00:25:04.280
So in real hardware,
Microsoft mostly drove it.

00:25:04.280 --> 00:25:06.680
And they got a key
baked into the firmware

00:25:06.680 --> 00:25:09.690
for a lot of the
OEM hardware makers.

00:25:09.690 --> 00:25:11.310
And that key is a public key.

00:25:11.310 --> 00:25:12.060
And it's baked in.

00:25:12.060 --> 00:25:13.310
And in the Google
case, we control it.

00:25:13.310 --> 00:25:14.230
So we have our key.

00:25:14.230 --> 00:25:17.420
And then the private
key is held by Microsoft

00:25:17.420 --> 00:25:20.510
or the distribution
owners or [INAUDIBLE]..

00:25:20.510 --> 00:25:23.540
Or in our case, we have a copy
for our container optimized

00:25:23.540 --> 00:25:24.410
image.

00:25:24.410 --> 00:25:27.540
And the people who make those
images sign those images.

00:25:27.540 --> 00:25:30.410
So they have their own process
for keeping those keys secure.

00:25:30.410 --> 00:25:32.210
They can have
those keys offline.

00:25:32.210 --> 00:25:35.930
They can be a quorum of three or
five people to do the signing.

00:25:35.930 --> 00:25:37.810
However secure they
want to keep those keys,

00:25:37.810 --> 00:25:39.720
they can make those very secure.

00:25:39.720 --> 00:25:42.160
So that public cryptokey
is baked into our firmware.

00:25:42.160 --> 00:25:46.010
Then the boot loader is
signed by that private key.

00:25:46.010 --> 00:25:47.912
When the Google controlled
firmware comes up,

00:25:47.912 --> 00:25:49.370
the first thing
it's going to do is

00:25:49.370 --> 00:25:51.440
it's going to check the
cryptographic signature

00:25:51.440 --> 00:25:52.630
on that boot loader.

00:25:52.630 --> 00:25:55.400
And if the signature does not
match or if it's been modified,

00:25:55.400 --> 00:25:56.450
it won't boot.

00:25:56.450 --> 00:25:58.400
Then the boot loader
then checks the signature

00:25:58.400 --> 00:25:59.840
on the operating system.

00:25:59.840 --> 00:26:03.120
It's up to the operating system
just how much to measure there.

00:26:03.120 --> 00:26:06.120
Most of them measure of the
entire kernel and the drivers,

00:26:06.120 --> 00:26:08.190
and if those don't
match as well.

00:26:08.190 --> 00:26:10.340
So if an attacker
has gone in and he's

00:26:10.340 --> 00:26:11.870
changed your kernel
drivers, which

00:26:11.870 --> 00:26:13.130
is where you want
to change things

00:26:13.130 --> 00:26:15.338
if you want to lie to all
of your intrusion detection

00:26:15.338 --> 00:26:18.560
systems and monitoring
systems, then secure boot

00:26:18.560 --> 00:26:20.850
will fail to boot.

00:26:20.850 --> 00:26:23.780
So another thing that's
often confused with it,

00:26:23.780 --> 00:26:25.940
but totally different,
is measured boot.

00:26:25.940 --> 00:26:27.170
They go hand in hand.

00:26:27.170 --> 00:26:30.170
Measure boot relies
on the virtual TPM.

00:26:30.170 --> 00:26:32.630
Again, TPM is a standard
that's been around a long time.

00:26:32.630 --> 00:26:35.500
As Nelly said, we are completely
compliant with the spec.

00:26:35.500 --> 00:26:37.920
It's been around,
again, about 20 years.

00:26:37.920 --> 00:26:41.210
So secure boot solves
a problem that says,

00:26:41.210 --> 00:26:45.749
am I in a state that has been
signed by someone, yes or no.

00:26:45.749 --> 00:26:46.790
And if yes, you can boot.

00:26:46.790 --> 00:26:47.960
If no, no.

00:26:47.960 --> 00:26:50.630
Measured boot tries to answer
the question of exactly what

00:26:50.630 --> 00:26:52.070
state did I boot in.

00:26:52.070 --> 00:26:56.360
And it tries to answer it in a
way so that after you've booted

00:26:56.360 --> 00:26:59.030
you can ask and be
confident about the answer.

00:26:59.030 --> 00:27:01.850
Because you can have a boot
log, Windows has a boot log,

00:27:01.850 --> 00:27:04.730
Linux has a boot log, and you
can go and read that boot log.

00:27:04.730 --> 00:27:07.430
But if someone's broken into
your kernel, how much trust can

00:27:07.430 --> 00:27:09.420
you really put
into that boot log?

00:27:09.420 --> 00:27:13.310
So TVMs have hardware mechanisms
called Platform Configuration

00:27:13.310 --> 00:27:16.280
Registers, so PCRs, and
the property of those

00:27:16.280 --> 00:27:20.450
is once you write to them,
they actually can't be zeroed.

00:27:20.450 --> 00:27:22.130
They can only be extended.

00:27:22.130 --> 00:27:24.770
So as the Google controlled
firmware is coming up,

00:27:24.770 --> 00:27:28.700
it reads the boot loader
and extends that into a PCR.

00:27:28.700 --> 00:27:31.730
Various other things like
the secure boot configuration

00:27:31.730 --> 00:27:33.250
are put into that PCR.

00:27:33.250 --> 00:27:35.750
And then the boot loader will
measure the operating system

00:27:35.750 --> 00:27:39.220
and put that in the PCR along
with a variety of other things.

00:27:39.220 --> 00:27:42.920
And what that provides you is
if you read those PCRs later,

00:27:42.920 --> 00:27:44.780
and they match, you
can be absolutely

00:27:44.780 --> 00:27:48.260
sure that has come up
in the configuration

00:27:48.260 --> 00:27:49.430
that you expected.

00:27:49.430 --> 00:27:51.920
If they don't match, you don't
know exactly what was wrong.

00:27:51.920 --> 00:27:54.290
You have a hint because
there's different PCRs

00:27:54.290 --> 00:27:55.530
for different things.

00:27:55.530 --> 00:27:58.249
But if they do match, you
can be absolutely certain--

00:27:58.249 --> 00:27:59.790
unless someone's
broken [INAUDIBLE],,

00:27:59.790 --> 00:28:01.790
but that's not very likely--

00:28:01.790 --> 00:28:03.460
that it came up in that state.

00:28:03.460 --> 00:28:07.460
The other thing that VTPM has
is because the VTPM participates

00:28:07.460 --> 00:28:09.680
in the cryptographic
protocol, it can remotely

00:28:09.680 --> 00:28:11.720
prove that to somebody else.

00:28:11.720 --> 00:28:14.300
So you can remotely
prove to another system

00:28:14.300 --> 00:28:17.180
that your system came up in
a good and measured state.

00:28:17.180 --> 00:28:19.520
And it was the right state.

00:28:19.520 --> 00:28:22.910
And in our case, you can
do the remote attestation

00:28:22.910 --> 00:28:25.640
with the VTPM or we
actually short circuit

00:28:25.640 --> 00:28:27.779
that to make it easier
because we control the cloud

00:28:27.779 --> 00:28:30.320
that you're running on top of
to deliver that straight to you

00:28:30.320 --> 00:28:33.960
via Stackdriver, as she showed
in the integrity monitoring

00:28:33.960 --> 00:28:35.390
demo.

00:28:35.390 --> 00:28:37.585
So VTPMs, as Nelly
was saying, I've said,

00:28:37.585 --> 00:28:39.710
they've been around a long
time so there's actually

00:28:39.710 --> 00:28:41.330
quite a few use cases.

00:28:41.330 --> 00:28:43.400
She talked about sort
of strong integrity.

00:28:43.400 --> 00:28:44.946
She talked about
protecting secrets,

00:28:44.946 --> 00:28:46.320
protecting
cryptographic secrets.

00:28:46.320 --> 00:28:50.620
There's use cases around tamper
evident logging and quite

00:28:50.620 --> 00:28:51.400
a few others.

00:28:51.400 --> 00:28:54.400
I'm really just going to talk
about the binding and sealing

00:28:54.400 --> 00:28:56.224
use case.

00:28:56.224 --> 00:28:57.640
It's one of the
more popular ones.

00:28:57.640 --> 00:28:59.880
It's used pretty
thoroughly in Windows.

00:28:59.880 --> 00:29:02.389
There are some Linux
applications that use it.

00:29:02.389 --> 00:29:03.430
Let's say you have a key.

00:29:03.430 --> 00:29:06.010
And you need to hold onto
that key across reboots

00:29:06.010 --> 00:29:08.710
because it encrypts something
or it saves something important.

00:29:08.710 --> 00:29:10.109
And you want to keep it around.

00:29:10.109 --> 00:29:11.650
But you don't want
to have it leaked.

00:29:11.650 --> 00:29:15.250
So it seems like a
pretty common use case.

00:29:15.250 --> 00:29:17.890
If someone compromises your
VM, reads all your files,

00:29:17.890 --> 00:29:19.900
or something gets a
backup of your disk,

00:29:19.900 --> 00:29:22.900
you don't want that key going
out, running around everywhere.

00:29:22.900 --> 00:29:25.420
So that's kind of what the
binding use case is for.

00:29:25.420 --> 00:29:28.060
So basically, you generate
your key or you take your key

00:29:28.060 --> 00:29:30.880
and you tell a TPM,
bind to this TPM.

00:29:30.880 --> 00:29:32.950
So it will encrypt
that key in such a way

00:29:32.950 --> 00:29:35.150
that only the TPM
can decrypt it.

00:29:35.150 --> 00:29:37.030
And because the TPM is
part of the hardware,

00:29:37.030 --> 00:29:39.490
that means you have to be on
that machine and in our case

00:29:39.490 --> 00:29:42.224
that virtual machine
to get that key back.

00:29:42.224 --> 00:29:43.390
That's the binding use case.

00:29:43.390 --> 00:29:45.244
If some backup
administrator, let's

00:29:45.244 --> 00:29:47.410
say you have a backup
administrator who takes backup

00:29:47.410 --> 00:29:49.690
of your systems, tries to
connect it to another VM

00:29:49.690 --> 00:29:52.720
or tries to exfiltrate
that data in any other way,

00:29:52.720 --> 00:29:54.490
your key is safe.

00:29:54.490 --> 00:29:56.849
A little bit more advanced
is actually sealing.

00:29:56.849 --> 00:29:58.390
So I talked about
measured boot and I

00:29:58.390 --> 00:30:01.450
talked about how it will measure
the state of your system.

00:30:01.450 --> 00:30:04.480
You can actually seal a
key to that measurement.

00:30:04.480 --> 00:30:07.730
So what that means is
that you take your key,

00:30:07.730 --> 00:30:09.250
the TPM will encrypt it.

00:30:09.250 --> 00:30:11.290
And it will only
decrypt it later

00:30:11.290 --> 00:30:13.480
if you booted in
the right state.

00:30:13.480 --> 00:30:15.970
So if you got some kernel
rootkit in there and someone

00:30:15.970 --> 00:30:18.850
has changed your OS system and
you try to get that key out,

00:30:18.850 --> 00:30:20.200
that won't work.

00:30:20.200 --> 00:30:21.940
This is one of the
major use cases.

00:30:21.940 --> 00:30:23.340
This is how BitLocker works.

00:30:23.340 --> 00:30:26.095
So BitLocker says, if you turn
off secure boot features that

00:30:26.095 --> 00:30:28.720
are part of this measurement and
my system doesn't come up with

00:30:28.720 --> 00:30:31.270
those features enabled, you
cannot recover your BitLocker

00:30:31.270 --> 00:30:33.080
keys, for example.

00:30:33.080 --> 00:30:36.190
If you were to take the entire
disk and move it somewhere else

00:30:36.190 --> 00:30:38.360
and try to decrypt
it, you would fail

00:30:38.360 --> 00:30:42.080
to do even though you got
the entire disk because

00:30:42.080 --> 00:30:44.410
of the BitLocker key is exposed.

00:30:44.410 --> 00:30:46.660
So now I'm going to hand
it over to Raphael and Mike

00:30:46.660 --> 00:30:50.244
from Gemalto to talk about
some customer success stories.

00:30:50.244 --> 00:30:54.627
[APPLAUSE]

00:30:54.627 --> 00:30:55.980
RAPHAEL DE CORMIS: Thanks, Andy.

00:30:55.980 --> 00:30:57.630
Thanks, Andy.

00:30:57.630 --> 00:31:03.270
So at Gemalto, we are a
security company from France.

00:31:03.270 --> 00:31:06.090
Maybe my accent told you that.

00:31:06.090 --> 00:31:09.030
And we are historically
providing security

00:31:09.030 --> 00:31:13.170
for SIM cards, payment
cards, or identity documents.

00:31:13.170 --> 00:31:16.110
And we have long also
moved to services

00:31:16.110 --> 00:31:20.640
in the cloud for our customers
around digital identity as well

00:31:20.640 --> 00:31:22.260
as authentication services.

00:31:26.730 --> 00:31:31.020
Moving to the cloud,
it's not an easy ride.

00:31:31.020 --> 00:31:32.850
And it's a bit like
going to the fun park.

00:31:32.850 --> 00:31:35.460
So to really enjoy it,
you have to make sure

00:31:35.460 --> 00:31:40.260
that you can trust the
ride and all the partners

00:31:40.260 --> 00:31:42.210
in the [INAUDIBLE].

00:31:42.210 --> 00:31:45.690
So we do have solutions
to encrypt at rest

00:31:45.690 --> 00:31:47.670
the data, to product
the data at rest,

00:31:47.670 --> 00:31:50.070
to protect the data in transit.

00:31:50.070 --> 00:31:53.850
And really, we wanted to
go further and protect it

00:31:53.850 --> 00:31:55.440
while it's in use.

00:31:55.440 --> 00:31:58.740
So as we are working with
Google on numerous topics

00:31:58.740 --> 00:32:02.470
such as Android security,
machine learning,

00:32:02.470 --> 00:32:04.720
we really accepted
the proposal of Nelly

00:32:04.720 --> 00:32:12.000
to go further and see what
virtual TPM could provide us.

00:32:12.000 --> 00:32:15.660
So why, really, should we
care about virtual TPM?

00:32:15.660 --> 00:32:18.060
So the first one is
that you can have

00:32:18.060 --> 00:32:22.530
your data being accessed while
being processed or in memory.

00:32:22.530 --> 00:32:24.600
And we want that
to be protected.

00:32:24.600 --> 00:32:28.470
Second is that you can have some
code which is not permission

00:32:28.470 --> 00:32:31.080
being executed on your VM.

00:32:31.080 --> 00:32:33.630
And that's not
something also you want.

00:32:33.630 --> 00:32:39.000
And finally, you have some
code that can be modified

00:32:39.000 --> 00:32:44.400
and which lead to bad execution
or to counterproductive measure

00:32:44.400 --> 00:32:47.440
in your service
that you don't want.

00:32:47.440 --> 00:32:53.360
So we did test, thanks to the
early access program, the VTPM

00:32:53.360 --> 00:32:54.540
and the shielded VM.

00:32:54.540 --> 00:32:58.860
And here is our very, let's
say, high level conclusions.

00:32:58.860 --> 00:33:02.310
So first of all, as
Nelly and Andy shown,

00:33:02.310 --> 00:33:04.050
it's a straightforward
configuration.

00:33:04.050 --> 00:33:09.030
So it was really very easy
to upgrade our workloads

00:33:09.030 --> 00:33:13.410
on our machines to be
sure that they would

00:33:13.410 --> 00:33:17.310
be running using shielded OSes.

00:33:17.310 --> 00:33:20.980
So second, we have
security teams in France.

00:33:20.980 --> 00:33:22.440
So they played all along.

00:33:22.440 --> 00:33:26.460
They tried every, let's say,
tricks that they would know

00:33:26.460 --> 00:33:28.860
and really, every
time, we were not

00:33:28.860 --> 00:33:32.850
able to stop any VM that was
attacked, either compromised

00:33:32.850 --> 00:33:36.240
with a malware and rootkit,
either on the Linux

00:33:36.240 --> 00:33:38.370
or on Windows.

00:33:38.370 --> 00:33:40.620
And finally, most
important, is that it

00:33:40.620 --> 00:33:43.590
has zero impact on
performance, and which

00:33:43.590 --> 00:33:48.690
is really helping out especially
when scaling down or up.

00:33:48.690 --> 00:33:51.430
So in term of usage, we see two.

00:33:51.430 --> 00:33:55.050
So the first one is really
one related to certification,

00:33:55.050 --> 00:33:59.430
meaning that for example on
the PCI DSS, which is a payment

00:33:59.430 --> 00:34:03.220
certification that we
take for our customer,

00:34:03.220 --> 00:34:06.990
there is some requirements
on the integrity

00:34:06.990 --> 00:34:08.980
of the data on the software.

00:34:08.980 --> 00:34:10.560
So this is really helping.

00:34:10.560 --> 00:34:13.380
And second, we also have
some very sensitive workload

00:34:13.380 --> 00:34:16.530
we want to protect, especially
around cryptography.

00:34:16.530 --> 00:34:18.800
And that's what Mike
will present you

00:34:18.800 --> 00:34:23.940
on all the very quick ways
we can get functions of VM.

00:34:23.940 --> 00:34:25.210
Thank you.

00:34:25.210 --> 00:34:29.440
[APPLAUSE]

00:34:29.440 --> 00:34:31.087
MIKE SCHROCK: So,
thanks, Raphael.

00:34:31.087 --> 00:34:31.670
