00:34:39.650 --> 00:34:41.260
And what we envision
and how we know

00:34:41.260 --> 00:34:43.139
our customers are
using our products

00:34:43.139 --> 00:34:44.830
and what they want
out of our products

00:34:44.830 --> 00:34:48.070
when they move to clouds
in a more virtual world.

00:34:48.070 --> 00:34:52.210
So Nelly, Andy, and
everybody talked

00:34:52.210 --> 00:34:54.699
about how the underlying
infrastructure can

00:34:54.699 --> 00:34:59.470
be highly secured now in new
ways with great attestation,

00:34:59.470 --> 00:35:02.710
so the hypervisor, the VMs.

00:35:02.710 --> 00:35:04.570
But in a shared
responsibility model

00:35:04.570 --> 00:35:06.390
from a security
perspective, you guys also

00:35:06.390 --> 00:35:07.870
have to architect up the stack.

00:35:07.870 --> 00:35:08.470
Right?

00:35:08.470 --> 00:35:10.390
So that becomes key management.

00:35:10.390 --> 00:35:11.920
That becomes secret management.

00:35:11.920 --> 00:35:15.346
It becomes DevOp security
policy, access controls,

00:35:15.346 --> 00:35:16.220
and things like that.

00:35:16.220 --> 00:35:22.780
So for 15, 20 years, Gemalto,
through our KeySecure product,

00:35:22.780 --> 00:35:25.090
has been delivering
that on premise side.

00:35:25.090 --> 00:35:27.844
Now we have a virtual
edition of KeySecure.

00:35:27.844 --> 00:35:29.260
And we want to
make sure that that

00:35:29.260 --> 00:35:32.500
gets deployed in the most
secure fashion on every cloud

00:35:32.500 --> 00:35:33.400
out there.

00:35:33.400 --> 00:35:36.490
And Google has offered
us an amazing ability

00:35:36.490 --> 00:35:39.250
to do that with shielded VM.

00:35:39.250 --> 00:35:42.730
And additionally,
that KeySecure,

00:35:42.730 --> 00:35:46.450
whether its tied back to on
premise physical KeySecures

00:35:46.450 --> 00:35:51.280
or its just running native up
in the cloud for your cloud

00:35:51.280 --> 00:35:55.270
applications, it tends to also
need a client side encryption

00:35:55.270 --> 00:35:57.580
component that sits on
the application level.

00:35:57.580 --> 00:36:02.410
So to do tokenization of the
data, to do data encryption,

00:36:02.410 --> 00:36:06.470
to do file level encryption,
to do database encryption,

00:36:06.470 --> 00:36:09.070
there's oftentimes a
client connector component

00:36:09.070 --> 00:36:11.890
to the key manager, using
[INAUDIBLE] to talk back

00:36:11.890 --> 00:36:14.190
and forth in a secure fashion.

00:36:14.190 --> 00:36:17.020
And so having both
the application

00:36:17.020 --> 00:36:24.340
on a trusted OS, on a trusted
VM, on a trusted hypervisor

00:36:24.340 --> 00:36:27.820
provides incredible
integrity to our solutions

00:36:27.820 --> 00:36:30.970
for our customers, things
that they could rely on

00:36:30.970 --> 00:36:33.490
in our hardened appliance back
in the physical data center.

00:36:33.490 --> 00:36:36.160
And now they can essentially
rely on it the same.

00:36:36.160 --> 00:36:41.920
We're excited about where this
is going and where it can go.

00:36:41.920 --> 00:36:44.320
We're excited about
